# Admin edge IP-allowlist — Phase 15 (DRAFT / EXAMPLE, not active).
#
# The admin console is the highest-value attack surface in the system (full
# cross-tenant + billing + impersonation). This snippet restricts the admin
# endpoints to the team's known egress IPs at the edge, BEFORE a request ever
# reaches Django — defence in depth on top of the app-layer MFA + RBAC.
#
# ── HOW TO ACTIVATE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. Collect the team's static egress IPs / office CIDR / VPN exit ranges.
# 2. Replace the placeholders in the `admin_allowed` matcher below.
# 3. Fold these blocks into infra/caddy/Caddyfile (merge the matcher into the
#    api.stampn.net site block and the admin.stampn.net block), reload Caddy:
#       docker compose -f infra/compose.prod.yml exec caddy caddy reload \
#         --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
# 4. VERIFY from an allowed IP AND from a disallowed one before you walk away —
#    a wrong CIDR here locks the team out of the console. Keep SSH access as the
#    recovery path (you can always reload Caddy from the box).
#
# NOTE: `remote_ip` sees Caddy's own view of the client. Because the API sits
# behind this same Caddy, that is the real client IP here. If you ever put a
# CDN/LB in front, switch to `client_ip` (trusted-proxy aware) instead.

(admin_allowed) {
	# ↓↓↓ REPLACE with the team's real IPs / CIDRs before activating ↓↓↓
	@blocked not remote_ip 203.0.113.0/24 198.51.100.7 10.0.0.0/8
	respond @blocked "Forbidden" 403
}

# Option A — lock the admin API paths on api.stampn.net:
#   api.stampn.net {
#       import admin_allowed_paths
#       ...existing reverse_proxy...
#   }
(admin_allowed_paths) {
	@admin_api path /api/admin/*
	@admin_blocked {
		path /api/admin/*
		not remote_ip 203.0.113.0/24 198.51.100.7 10.0.0.0/8
	}
	respond @admin_blocked "Forbidden" 403
}

# Option B — lock the whole admin.stampn.net subdomain:
#   admin.stampn.net {
#       import admin_allowed
#       encode zstd gzip
#       ...existing config...
#   }
